---
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import Kicker from '../Kicker.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../SectionHeading.astro';
import Card from '../Card.astro';

const flowNodes = [
  ['REQUEST', 'incoming HTTP'],
  ['ROUTER', 'match route'],
  ['DI', 'resolve services'],
  ['CONTROLLER', 'your code'],
  ['RESPONSE', 'emit output'],
];
---

<Section id="why" tone="dark">
  <Kicker>The honest performance story</Kicker>
  <SectionHeading class="max-w-[760px]">Low overhead, by design.</SectionHeading>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-[720px] text-[17px] leading-[1.7] text-(--tone-muted)">
    Phalcon keeps a small, loosely-coupled object graph and a lean request lifecycle. Under a worker-mode runtime —
    <strong class="font-semibold text-mist-200">FrankenPHP</strong>,
    <strong class="font-semibold text-mist-200">RoadRunner</strong>,
    <strong class="font-semibold text-mist-200">Swoole</strong> — the framework boots once and stays resident, so
    bootstrap cost is paid at startup instead of per request.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-11 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line-700 bg-surface-950 px-[26px] py-[30px]">
    <div class="mb-[22px] flex min-w-[720px] items-center justify-between">
      <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-faint-500"
        >Request lifecycle, where overhead is saved</span
      >
      <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold text-faint-600">worker mode: boot once ↻ stay resident</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex min-w-[720px] items-stretch">
      {
        flowNodes.map((node, i) => (
          <>
            <div
              class="flex-1 border border-line-700 bg-surface-900 px-2 py-4 text-center"
              style="clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 100% 78%, 92% 100%, 0 100%);"
            >
              <div class="font-mono text-[13px] font-bold tracking-[0.06em] text-accent-400">{node[0]}</div>
              <div class="mt-[7px] font-mono text-[11px] font-medium leading-[1.3] text-faint-600">{node[1]}</div>
            </div>
            {i < flowNodes.length - 1 && (
              <div class="flex items-center px-[6px]" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="30" height="12" viewBox="0 0 30 12">
                  <line x1="0" y1="6" x2="22" y2="6" stroke="#3f5950" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
                  <path d="M22 2 L29 6 L22 10" fill="none" stroke="#74ccb2" stroke-width="1.5" />
                </svg>
              </div>
            )}
          </>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-[34px] grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
    <Card variant="accent" label="The tradeoff, stated plainly" labelColor="#8fc19a" barColor="#8fc19a">
      Pure PHP gives up the raw micro-benchmark edge of the compiled extension. What it buys: zero install friction, it
      runs on any host that runs PHP, static analysis works, and every method is readable and patchable in your own
      vendor directory.
    </Card>
    <Card variant="accent" label="On benchmarks" labelColor="#e2c37a" barColor="#e2c37a">
      v6 benchmarks are in progress. We publish reproducible methodology rather than reprinting 2012-era numbers — PHP
      8.x JIT helps CPU-bound work (+61–95%) but little on the I/O-bound requests most apps serve.
    </Card>
  </div>
</Section>
